      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The One and the Infinite</strong><br>\nPlotinus's concept of The One is abstract and beyond physical existence. The One is infinite and undivided, beyond all forms of being and thought. It is not just the creator of the universe but also its essence. Plotinus taught that by understanding The One, people can aspire to a state of pure existence and thought.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Emanation: The Universe from The One</strong><br>\nThe process by which everything exists, according to Plotinus, is emanation, not creation. He explained that from The One emanates the Divine Intellect, from which the Soul of the world originates. This emanative process continues down to the level of individual human souls and the material world, which is the last in the chain of emanation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Divine Intellect</strong><br>\nPlotinus asserted that the Divine Intellect is the way in which The One reflects upon itself and contains all the ideas or forms that Plato spoke of. These forms are eternal truths that stand as the templates for every kind of thing that exists in the material world. The intellect is the first distinct 'thing' that emanates from The One.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
